<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies show the promise of large language models (LLMs) for few-shot tabular classification but highlight challenges due to the variability in structured data. To address this, we propose distilling data into actionable insights to enable robust and effective tabular classification by LLMs. Inspired from human learning processes, we introduce InsightTab, an insight distillation framework guided by principles of divide-and-conquer, easy-first, and reflective learning. It integrates rule summarization, strategic exemplification, and insight reflection through deep collaboration between LLMs and data modeling techniques. The obtained insights enable LLMs to better align their general knowledge and capabilities with the particular requirements of specific tabular tasks. We extensively evaluate InsightTab on nine datasets. The results demonstrate consistent improvement over state-of-the-art methods. Ablation studies further validate the principle-guided distillation process, and in-depth analyses emphasize InsightTab’s effectiveness in leveraging labeled data and managing biases.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tabular data is widely used across applications due to the prevalence of relational databases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shwartz-Ziv and Armon (2021)</a></cite>, inspiring tasks like tabular question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vakulenko and Savenkov (2017)</a></cite>, retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a></cite>, etc.
Among these tasks, tabular classification plays a key role by categorizing tabular data with numerical and categorical features into predefined classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional tabular classification tasks leverage ensemble techniques with decision trees such as XGBoost to handle mixed numerical and categorical data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shwartz-Ziv and Armon (2021)</a></cite>. The high cost of labeling large datasets and feature engineering has driven interest in few-shot learning, where models make accurate predictions using only a few examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hegselmann et al. (2022)</a></cite>. These approaches often leverage large language models (LLMs), which enhance prediction accuracy by transforming structured data into natural language sequences, enabling LLMs to process and make informed predictions. They show that combining proper serialization with LLMs achieves strong performance, with later studies refining this through expert and algorithmic prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Slack and Singh (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manikandan et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, several challenges persist for effective LLM-based tabular classification in the few-shot setting. These include:

<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <em id="S1.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Filling knowledge gap</em><span id="S1.I1.i1.3" class="ltx_text">. Tabular data often includes task-specific details (e.g., task description), failing to incorporate them can hinder the performance of general-purpose LLMs.
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <em id="S1.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Unlocking LLMs’ full potential</em><span id="S1.I1.i2.3" class="ltx_text">. Effective tabular classification requires complex processing. While methods like in-context learning help, they fall short of fully utilizing LLMs’ capabilities, making this an ongoing challenge.
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <em id="S1.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Balancing performance and cost</em><span id="S1.I1.i3.3" class="ltx_text">. Tabular classification tasks are critical for real-time services, demanding fast response times and cost efficiency. Traditional methods, while effective, are often time-intensive to train. Thus, LLM-based solutions must be both efficient and economical to meet these demands.</span></span>
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we focus on the task of <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-based tabular classification in the few-shot setting</span>. We pose a fundamental research question: <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can actionable insights be distilled from the training data to enhance LLMs’ performance in this task?</span> In response, we introduce InsightTab, a novel insight distillation framework that integrates traditional machine learning techniques with LLMs, enabling them to acquire task-specific knowledge from a data modeling perspective. In this analogy, LLMs can be seen as students with broad general knowledge but limited task-specific expertise.
Inspired by human learning processes, InsightTab employs three key principles: <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">divide-and-conquer</span>, <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">easy-first</span>, and <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reflective learning</span>. The <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">divide-and-conquer</span> strategy, inspired by curriculum learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. (2009)</a></cite>, partitions training data into subsets, enabling LLMs to derive rules for each, <em id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> a cognitive process akin to <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">summarization</span>. This could simultaneously avoid context window overflow and yield more targeted rules than directly feeding all data into an LLM. The <span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">easy-first</span> principle mimics the human cognitive procedure of <span id="S1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exemplifying</span>, prioritizing the selection of representative and simpler samples for initial learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Finally, the <span id="S1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reflective learning</span> strategy allows LLMs to engage in <span id="S1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reflection</span>, <em id="S1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> learning from misclassified cases to enhance their distilled insights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We then use three operators for the insight distillation process: <code id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">group</code> for clustering similar samples, <code id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">rank</code> for example ordering with prediction difficulty, and <code id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">summarize</code> for extracting natural language rules. This process fosters collaboration between data modeling methods and LLMs, leveraging LLMs’ strength in summarization while addressing their limitation in data analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We validate InsightTab through experiments on nine diverse datasets, demonstrating its superiority over state-of-the-art methods in average performance. Additionally, InsightTab outperforms other approaches using the same base LLMs in 19 out of 20 comparisons. This highlights the importance of insight distillation for few-shot tabular classification. Ablation and case studies further confirm the effectiveness of each module, and tests on biased data show InsightTab’s priority in robustness, reducing overfitting and improving generalization. In summary, our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conceptually, we highlight the importance of insight distillation through a deep integration of data modeling methods and LLMs prompting for few-shot tabular classification.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Methodologically, we present InsightTab, an insight distillation framework inspired by human learning principles of divide-and-conquer, easy-first, and reflective learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experimentally, we validate the enhanced effectiveness of InsightTab through extensive tests, as well as its rationales and superiority in sample efficiency and robustness<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Code and dataset available at <a href="https://github.com/yfyuan01/InsightTab/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/yfyuan01/InsightTab/</a>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2508.21561v1/tabular-framework.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/252;" width="476" height="252" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Framework overview of InsightTab. (a) We present an income prediction dataset with <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> samples and <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> features.
The text template serialization converts column features into natural language sequences, while the multifaceted serialization further integrates them with task-specific knowledge into a prompt, for LLMs to predict test data. (b) Insight distillation involves both data modeling techniques and LLMs for sample grouping/ranking and rule summarization, respectively. The process is guided by three principles inspired from human learning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tabular classification</span>.
Tabular classification assigns labels to table rows based on column attributes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Traditional methods use gradient-boosted decision trees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Guestrin (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. (2017)</a></cite> for structured data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shwartz-Ziv and Armon (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gorishniy et al. (2021)</a></cite>, while others design specialized architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2021)</a></cite>, such as TabularNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2021)</a></cite>, which encodes spatial and relational information.
Transformer-based approaches have also been explored <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nassar et al. (2022)</a></cite>, including models specifically optimized for small tabular datasets like TabPFN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al. (2022)</a></cite>. Recently, LLMs have gained attention for their few-shot learning abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite>, enabling classification with minimal samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hegselmann et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al. (2023)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al. (2025)</a></cite> incorporate meta-learning and curriculum learning into LLM prompting, drawing inspiration from traditional data mining techniques.
Based on that, our approach
focuses on LLM-based few-shot tabular classification and propose to distill actionable insights from tabular data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs for tabular data</span>.
Language models have demonstrated effective capabilities in handling structured data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Extensive studies have explored pretraining language models to effectively learn structured table representations, ranging from smaller-scale pretrained models (<em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> BERT, GPT-2) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iida et al. (2021)</a></cite> to state-of-the-art LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zha et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For example,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2023)</a></cite> fine-tune Llama 2 to develop open-source LLMs capable of handling a variety of table-based tasks.
Since LLMs are designed for natural language, converting tabular data into text enables them to analyze and generate insights effectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al. (2023)</a></cite>. This process, known as data serialization, typically involves linearizing tables row by row with column separators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singha et al. (2023)</a></cite>. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite> find that creating in-context learning prompts which include instruction helps improve Text-to-SQL performance. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manikandan et al. (2023)</a></cite> are the first to generate summaries and shows that incorporating task-specific knowledge during serialization can improve tabular classification. Building on their findings, our work aims to develop a multifaceted serialization prompt that combines examples and rules from the training samples in natural language format, providing a specialized form of serialization. This approach provides strong guidance for LLMs, helping them understand the data structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the structure of InsightTab, which integrates data modeling techniques with LLMs for better tabular classification. An overview of our approach is presented in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Summarize-Exemplify-Reflect: Data-driven Insight Distillation Empowers LLMs for Few-shot Tabular Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tabular Data</span>.
A labeled tabular dataset with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rows (<em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> samples) and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> columns (<em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> features) could be denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional feature vector and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(i)}\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(i)}\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding class label of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of labels for a tabular classification task <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Each column of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a semantically meaningful feature name such as age, education, or marital status. These feature names are formally denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\{f_{1},\dots,f_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\{f_{1},\dots,f_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multifaceted Serialization</span>.
A prerequisite for using LLMs in tabular classification is data serialization, which involves converting raw tabular data into natural language to enhance LLM understanding.
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hegselmann et al. (2022)</a></cite> empirically examined nine different serialization formats for tabular data and found that the <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Text Template</em> approach performs well across their experiments. Specifically, this approach converts a feature vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to a textual enumeration of all features as “The <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. … The <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.”, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th value of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Summarize-Exemplify-Reflect: Data-driven Insight Distillation Empowers LLMs for Few-shot Tabular Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We adopt it as the default sample-level serialization method.
Additionally, concurrent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Slack and Singh (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manikandan et al. (2023)</a></cite> have verified that LLMs benefit from task-specific knowledge for tabular classification. The knowledge includes but not limited to task background, labeled few-shot examples, and rules mined from data. We hence extend the scope of serialization and define a multifaceted serialization prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{MSP}(x,T,\mathcal{D}_{s},R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MSP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{MSP}(x,T,\mathcal{D}_{s},R)</annotation></semantics></math> which converts not only test sample <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> but also the task <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, few-shot in-context learning examples <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{s}\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{s}\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and mined rules <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to natural language prompt. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Summarize-Exemplify-Reflect: Data-driven Insight Distillation Empowers LLMs for Few-shot Tabular Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> also demonstrates a brief example of it (prompts are listed in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E LLM Prompts ‣ Summarize-Exemplify-Reflect: Data-driven Insight Distillation Empowers LLMs for Few-shot Tabular Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Formalization</span>.
With the aforementioned concepts, we formalize the LLM-based tabular classification task, particularly in few-shot settings. Given the tabular classification task <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, an associated labeled dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an unlabeled test sample <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, the task goal is to predict a class label <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with an LLM. This can be expressed as <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}=\texttt{LLM}_{p}(\texttt{MSP}(\tilde{x},T,\mathcal{D}_{s},R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MSP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}=\texttt{LLM}_{p}(\texttt{MSP}(\tilde{x},T,\mathcal{D}_{s},R))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> represents the rules mined by the LLM from the training samples.
These few-shot settings often occur in realistic scenarios where the labeled data is limited and has complex patterns. In these cases, LLMs can effectively leverage their rich language knowledge to make accurate predictions, whereas traditional methods struggle to fit an effective decision function with limited data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Collaborative Insight Distillation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a method InsightTab that enables LLMs to approach the task from a data modeling perspective and distill training data into actionable <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">insights</em>. These insights are injected into the multifaceted serialization function of InsightTab, enabling LLMs to better adapt their general natural language understanding capabilities to different tabular prediction tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this context, the role of LLMs could be an analogy to students with extensive general knowledge but limited expertise in the specific task.
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Summarize-Exemplify-Reflect: Data-driven Insight Distillation Empowers LLMs for Few-shot Tabular Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, drawing from human learning strategies, we employ three key principles, namely <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">divide-and-conquer</em>, <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">easy-first</em>, and <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reflective learning</em>, to optimize the learning process for LLMs.
Specifically, we adopt a divide-and-conquer strategy for rule summarization: training samples are firstly grouped into subsets of ‘similar’ examples. Summarizing rules for each subset individually then becomes more manageable than for the entire dataset.
Next, following the easy-first principle, we choose the most confident samples as in-context demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al. (2024)</a></cite>. This allows LLMs to quickly acquire skills when confronting a new tabular classification task.
Finally, we design a reflective learning mechanism for LLMs to refine their formed knowledge. In this mechanism, a subset of challenging examples is selected from the training data. LLMs are required to make predictions based on the existing rules and demonstrations. Incorrectly predicted samples are retained for further rule summarization, resulting in new rules that augment the existing ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, principle-guided insight distillation allows LLMs to draw task-specific knowledge from data, which ultimately enhances the overall tabular classification. To facilitate this insight distillation process, we employ three
key operators:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">group</span> divides samples into subsets with high intra-group similarity;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rank</span> orders samples based on the difficulty for prediction;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">summarize</span> derives a concise set of natural language rules from the samples.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In summary, while LLMs excel at <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">summarize</span>, they may fall short in the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">group</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rank</span> operations which involve complex mathematical calculations, reasoning, and iterative refinement—tasks that are difficult to articulate clearly with natural language instructions.
Conversely, <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">group</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rank</span> correspond to well-defined data modeling tasks that traditional machine learning methods handle effectively. Therefore, InsightTab forsters a collaboration between LLMs and data modeling, enabling effective insight distillation for LLMs. Details are introduced in the next section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
